    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d31">

                <pb facs="0034" n="22" xml:id="pg22"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860D.6359 International Nickel Company/30:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Finland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schoenfeld</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-26T15:00:00+02:00">April 26, 1941—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>109. President Ryti informed me today that the Soviet Government had expressed
                    readiness to continue Petsamo nickel negotiations in Helsinki through the new
                    Soviet Minister, Orlov, who presented credentials April 24. Orlov was described
                    by the President as more amenable than his predecessor<anchor n="63"
                        xml:id="d31-f1"/> and the Finns seem hopeful that progress can be made with
                    the new Minister.</p>
                <p>Ryti said Finnish Government was withholding latest memorandum which had been
                    sent to its Legation at Moscow on nickel question pending result of proposed
                    conversations here. Finnish Government, he said, stands on refusal to turn
                    management and operation of the mine to Russians but, as I gathered, remains
                    disposed to grant participation and perhaps control in suggested holding company
                    leaving operating company in position to turn over product to Russians and
                    Germans more or less in proportions previously contemplated.</p>
                <p>Copy to Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schoenfeld</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d31-f1" n="63" xml:id="d31-f1-content">
                <p>P. G. Orlov had succeeded Ivan S. Zotov.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
